erstood it certainly  doesn't hurt to up your intake of inulin  fiber to support a healthy gut in  general immunity but to make a  definitive statement that a particular  product will protect you against corona  virus is just so grossly wrong if you're  interested in improving your gut health  to support general immunity you can do  so by adding more probiotic rich foods  to your diet like yogurt Kim  Mesa or sauerkraut you can also  incorporate more prebiotic fibers into  your meals so foods like asparagus  garlic bananas onions or chicory root  and doing all of that of course while  aiming towards eating more plant-based  foods that are rich in fiber and  antioxidants you don't need a $30  supplement to do that next and finally  let's talk about vitamin mega dosing now  you may or may not have heard about  members of the wellness community such  as Peter Osborne and Andrew Saul  recommending mega doses of antioxidants  like vitamin A C and D as a means to  protect against Koba 19 not only are  these recommendations not supported by  the evidence but they may actually be  extremely dangerous these people are  recommending doses that are way beyond  the recommended amounts for each vitamin  and they pose some serious health risks  vitamin C for example does have a role  in immunity and wound healing and can be  obtained from the diet by eating a  variety of fruits and vegetables because  vitamin C is water-soluble it does not  get stored in the body and any excess is  extruded out in your piece however this  is mostly true for food sources of  vitamin C whereas supplemental forms of  vitamin C are not excreted as easily  from the body leading to an increased  risk of toxicity 1 2019 review did find  that oral and IV high doses of vitamin C  treatment may reduce ICU stay by 8% and  shortened ventillation duration by 18.2%  however in practice including early  experiments with Coppa 19 patients  doctors would never give these high  amounts orally they would need to be  administered through an IV very  carefully monitored by a doctor  the reason is that excess vitamin C  supplementation is not necessarily  benign access vitamin C supplementation  can cause adverse health effects ranging  from digestive symptoms like diarrhea  and nausea to the development of kidney  stones and it may also lead to iron  overload which can be seriously damaging  for organs like the heart liver and  pancreas  these adverse effects appear to occur  when people are taking vitamin C doses  just over 2,000 milligrams and our  friends Peter Osborne and Andrew soul  are recommending between 5,000 and  24,000 milligrams  folks you may not need to go to the ER  for kovat but you'll be praying they  have a bed when your liver fails as for  vitamin D low levels of vitamin D have  been associated with respiratory disease  as well as viral respiratory infections  and decreased lung function well we  don't have actual research on vitamin D  for kovat it does make sense that we  would want to keep our vitamin levels in  check especially considering the high  prevalence of vitamin D deficiency  worldwide but as for high single doses a  systematic review concluded that single  doses of vitamin D up to a hundred  thousand is may be effective at  improving vitamin D status to prevent  deficiency while under medical  supervision  my view these are findings not  recommendations and they pertain to a  single dose every three months not daily  dosages also similar to vitamin C  vitamin D also has potential risks from  high school mental doses including  elevated calcium levels digestive  symptoms dizziness confusion nausea  vomiting stomach pain bone loss and even  kidney failure toxicity is also more  likely to occur in people taking high  dose up laments as it is very unlikely  to reach high blood levels through diet  alone contrary to vitamin C vitamin D is  fat soluble meaning it's stored in the  body fat and released into the  bloodstream slowly versus being excrete